2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 22.47KB 15.45s
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Thank you for sharing these files. It’s a fascinating collection of work-in-progress code and
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #design notes for what looks like a cellular automaton / maze‑improvisation system that combines
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bitmap grids, tile‑based rendering, keyboard control, and even a traffic‑stream simulator.
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #A few observations and questions that might help move the project forward:
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Maze.md – The embedded diagrams show a neat two‑state generator (`▤` → `▥▢▣▣▣▢`, `▥` →
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`▤▢▢▢▢▢`) and a right‑shifting rule for cars (`▢▣▣▣▢ → ▢▢▣▣▣`). This is very reminiscent of
2225b180 [deepseek-v4-flash] #rule‑based traffic systems or rule 184, but adapted to 2D. The repeated indentation pattern
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #(diagonally stacked copies) suggests you’re considering a toroidal or staggered world. Is the goal
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #to simulate a moving “train” of cars through a maze that also regenerates itself?
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Grid.ts – You’ve implemented a bitmap with quad‑pixel rendering (`▘▝▀▖▌▞▛…`), a
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #scrollable window, and inertia‑based panning (via arrow keys). The `gridWindow()` function maps 2×2
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #blocks to quadrants, which works nicely for low‑resolution display. The pump‑based acceleration and
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #decay give smooth camera movement. Are you planning to overlay maze tiles on this bitmap, or is
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #this a separate viewer for another data source?
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Maze.ts – The `outlineGrid` / `boxIndex` function attempts to replace the generic `▢`/`▣`
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #symbols with box‑drawing characters derived from a 3×3 neighbourhood. That’s a strong start, but I
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #see some patterns returning an `-1` (logged as an error). The `swizzle` and replacement logic in
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`draw()` is creative – using random permutations of grass symbols to break up long runs of `▣`.
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Would you like help improving the ambiguity matching (e.g., handling more corner/tee variants, or
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #using a lookup table of all 512 patterns)?
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Terminal.ts & Traffic.ts – These provide the keyboard polling framework. Traffic.ts shows
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #a 1‑D lane version of the same idea (generator + movement). That’s a nice proof‑of‑concept; the 2D
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #version in `maze.ts` can reuse the same `updateLane` approach.
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #If any part of this is new or different from your earlier plans, I’d be happy to discuss:
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. How to combine the bitmap viewer with the maze generation (e.g., rendering the maze grid as a
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #quadtree or directly into the bitmap).
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Optimising the neighbourhood sampling for larger mazes.
2225b181 [deepseek-v4-flash] #3. Adding a more systematic rule set (e.g., using a finite state machine for the generators).
2225b182 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2225b182 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Let me know what direction you’d like to take next.
2225b182 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
